## Approval Tiers

How findings are handled. The quality gate and the agents tag every finding with a tier. They never choose the action.

| Tier | Examples | Behaviour |
|------|----------|-----------|
| decide-and-log | Stale line references, naming, docs, tests for unreached branches, fixes inside touched files | The controller acts and records it in the ledger |
| batch | Deferred minors, follow-up candidates, design-review findings | Surfaced at a natural break, never a gate |
| needs-approval | Schema or migration changes, public API changes, new dependencies, new design tokens, {{PROTECTED_AREAS}}, departures from the spec or plan, scope additions, any new suppression | Only the dependent work waits. Independent work continues |
| never | A regression baseline going up, copying a legacy pattern into new code | Not approvable |
| stop | Irreversible or destructive operations, security-sensitive actions, side effects outside the worktree, a plan broken so that every path is a guess | Stop and ask |

### Gate Row Tiers

The quality gate's `tier` column classifies each row. The five tiers above are the approval policy.

| Gate row tier | Policy tier |
|---------------|-------------|
| `none` | No action needed |
| `decide-and-log` | decide-and-log |
| `needs-approval` | needs-approval |
| `never` | never. Not approvable, so fix the regression rather than ask |
| `controller-classifies` | The controller picks one of the five policy tiers for the row. Files outside the plan can be decide-and-log, batch, needs-approval, or stop |

`batch` and `stop` are policy-only tiers. The gate never emits them.

The legacy-patterns row is `decide-and-log` because pre-existing occurrences in a touched file are reported too; an occurrence on a line this branch added is `never`.
